# Build output and installed packages.
node_modules/
dist/

# Real environment values. .env.example is tracked and documents every key.
.env
.env.local
.env.*.local

# The service's leaderboard and challenge snapshot. Everything in it is public
# by design, but it is runtime state rather than source, so it stays out.
.data/
*.tmp

# Planning and internal docs. The public repo gets the README, which is written
# for someone arriving cold. PLAN.md is the build spec with its locked-decisions
# table, docs/ holds the submission checklist, the deploy runbook and the
# marketing plan. All useful, none of it anyone else's business.
PLAN.md

# Note the star. Git cannot re-include anything whose parent directory is
# excluded, so ignoring `docs/` outright would make the exception below silently
# do nothing. Excluding the CONTENTS leaves the directory itself visible, which
# is what lets one subfolder back in.
docs/*

# Screenshots are generated, never captured, so new ones do not enter the repo
# by accident. The twelve Atlas shots committed before this line stay tracked,
# because gitignore has no effect on a file git already knows about and the
# README embeds them. Regenerate with `npm run shoot:atlas` and stage the
# changed files deliberately.
docs/shots/

# The stage guide, which the README links to. It is the overflow for the part of
# the README that would otherwise be seven long sections, so it is as public as
# the README is. Without this line the link is a 404 for everybody but us, and
# nothing about the local checkout would show it.

# What we hit building on the Mini App SDK, linked from the README. It belongs
# in the public repo for the same reason the stage guide does: the README points
# at it, and a link that 404s for everybody but us is worse than no link. It is
# also the feedback the competition asks for, and feedback nobody can read is
# feedback nobody gave.
#
# It lives here rather than in docs/submit/ because that folder is the private
# side: the launch post, the marketing copy, the screenshots for the form. Git
# cannot re-include a file whose parent directory is excluded, so keeping it
# there would have meant un-ignoring the folder and re-ignoring its contents one
# by one, which is a rule that breaks the next time somebody adds a file.

# The 250 word description the submission portal asks for. Kept in the repo so
# the text that was submitted is the text in version control, rather than a
# paragraph that only ever existed in a form field.

# The player-facing how to play guide.
#
# This one was not un-ignored, and the cost was a red pipeline nobody could
# reproduce locally. tests/atlas-how-to-play.test.ts reads it at line 8, the
# file sat on every developer's disk, and `npm run check` was green on every
# machine while CI failed with ENOENT on a path that plainly existed. A test
# that depends on an ignored file can only ever pass where the file was
# written by hand.
!docs/nim-atlas-how-to-play.md

# Current NIM Atlas release docs and generated screenshots are public evidence.
# The older Cycle I plans remain ignored or explicitly marked historical, but
# these files are linked from the current README and must travel with it.
!docs/README.md
!docs/how-nim-rush-works.md
!docs/roadmap.md
!docs/nim-atlas-architecture.md
!docs/deploy.md
!docs/privacy.md
docs/submit/*
!docs/shots/
!docs/shots/*.png
!docs/shots/captions.json

# Editor and OS noise.
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
.vscode/
.idea/
*.log

# Local procedural-art review and measurement output. The build scripts and
# source specifications are the reproducible record; these files are local
# inspection aids and Python bytecode is disposable cache.
.atlas-3d-baseline.json
art-review.html
**/__pycache__/
*.py[cod]

# Stray dev-server output. Runtime noise, never source.
*.err

# package-lock.json is deliberately NOT ignored. Pinned installs are the whole
# point of committing it, and a mini app that builds differently on the judge's
# machine than on yours is a mini app you cannot debug.

PLAN-V2.md

# v3 planning. Private, like the other plans.
PLAN-V3.md

# v4: the survival contest. The kind exists and is refused at the door until
# the level behind it does, so this is a spec rather than a record.
PLAN-V4.md

# Deploy keys, and anything else shaped like a private key.
#
# A key was generated into this directory during CI setup and sat here untracked
# while `git add -A` ran several times. It was never committed, but only because
# of timing. A private key in a public repository is the worst single mistake
# available here, so the pattern is broad on purpose: it catches openssh keys,
# PEM files and the usual id_* names regardless of what anyone remembers to do.
sface-deploy
sface-deploy.pub
*.pem
*.key
id_ed25519*
id_rsa*

# Throwaway browser profile from scripts/shoot.mjs. Regenerated on every run and
# full of cache nobody wants in a diff.
.shoot-profile/
.atlas-shoot-profile/
.atlas-shoot-profile-*/
.atlas-measure-profile/
.atlas-contrast-profile/

# The launch video's working directory: captured frames, encoded shots, the
# narration clips and the export. Hundreds of megabytes of PNGs and MP4s that
# are all rebuildable from scripts/record.mjs, build-video.mjs and
# finish-video.mjs, so the recipe is tracked and the output is not.
.video/

# Playwright MCP inspection artifacts are local browser state, not source.
.playwright-mcp/

# Brainstorming visual companion session files are local design tooling output.
.superpowers/

# Internal Cycle 2 planning and review artifacts. These guide local agent work
# but are not part of the public competition repository.
docs/superpowers/
ui-artifacts/
PLAN-CYCLE-*.md

# Internal audits, plans, research notes and handoffs. Keep these local even
# when they live outside docs/superpowers; public product docs are named and
# re-included explicitly above.
*-audit-*.md
*-plan-*.md
*-planning-*.md
*-research-*.md
*-handoff-*.md
*AUDIT*.md
*PLAN*.md
*RESEARCH*.md
*HANDOFF*.md
docs/**/plans/
docs/**/planning/
docs/**/audits/
docs/**/handoffs/
docs/**/drafts/

# Ad-hoc verification screenshots dropped in the repo root while checking a
# change by hand. Rule: evidence is generated, never captured. The tracked
# recipe is scripts/shoot-atlas.mjs, which writes the real set into
# docs/shots/, and a hand-taken PNG beside it is a picture that cannot be
# regenerated and will disagree with the build the first time anything moves.
/sface-*-pass.png

# Vercel CLI local link state. Contains the project and org ids for this
# checkout, which are per-developer, so it never belongs to anyone else.
.vercel

# Every real environment file. Added broadly on purpose: the pattern has to
# catch .env.production and whatever the next platform names its file, not just
# the three that exist today.
#
# The star also swallows .env.example, which is the one file here that MUST be
# public. It is the only documentation of every key the service reads, and a
# fresh clone with no .env.example is a service nobody else can configure. It
# survived only because it was already tracked, and a tracked file ignores
# .gitignore. That is luck, not a rule, so the exception below makes it a rule.
.env*
!.env.example

# Local-only research, runtime exports, and character preview variants.
HACKATHON_RESEARCH.md
data/
art/atlas/characters/atlas-walker-v1/preview-front.png
art/atlas/characters/atlas-walker-v1/preview-turntable.png

# Internal agent/workspace state. Keep product source and public documentation tracked.
.codex/
.agents/

# Renders from the v2 character build: two rest-pose previews and four contact
# sheets per clip. They are how the character gets looked at rather than
# trusted, but they are 8.2 MB of regenerable output and the v1 previews above
# are ignored for the same reason. `npm run build:atlas:character:v2` writes
# them again on demand.
art/atlas/characters/atlas-walker-v2/*.png

# Working design document for the game's storyline. A scratchpad we edit
# together, not a shipped spec; it becomes real as scene data and code.
STORY-SCRIPT.md

# Throwaway Chrome profile from scripts/render-brand.mjs.
.brand-render-profile/

# Internal working copies.
#
# Submission checklists, portal copy, launch posts, planning specs and status
# notes are the work, not the product. They stay on the machine that wrote
# them. The public repository carries the README, the rules, the roadmap, the
# architecture, the deploy runbook and the privacy notes, and nothing else.
internal/
docs/submission.md
docs/description.md
docs/marketing.md
docs/feedback.md
docs/nim-atlas-current-state.md
docs/stage-1-to-7.md
docs/submit/
